# /etc/sudoers.d/bayram-deploy — the deploy account's unattended verbs on abdu-test.
# Install mode 0440, owner root:root.
#
# ============================================================================
# POST-CUTOVER. THIS FILE IS NOW THE WHOLE OF THE OPERATIONAL GRANT.
# ============================================================================
# Written 2026-09-11 as a TRANSITIONAL file naming both unit sets, with a standing instruction
# in its own header: "once §7's verification has passed and the rollback window is closed,
# delete the old file and the HBD_* halves below in one edit" (10-rename-cutover.md §4.7, §8.3).
# Verification passed 2026-09-14. This is that edit, made 2026-09-19, together with the
# removals the sudo survey of the same day measured.
#
# `/etc/sudoers.d/hbd-deploy` goes with it. The four verbs in that file that are still
# load-bearing are CADDY verbs, and they are re-homed below — which is why
# deploy/prune-stale-sudo-grants.sh installs THIS file before it touches that one, and refuses
# to delete it at all unless it can see the caddy verbs here first. That ordering is
# load-bearing, not stylistic: caddy.service is the local hop for pay.bayrambot.uz -> :8091
# and admin.bayrambot.uz -> :8080 behind the Cloudflare tunnel.
#
# THE hbd-* GRANTS WERE NOT DANGLING, AND A FUTURE READER MUST NOT "FIX" THAT.
# Measured on the host 2026-09-19: hbd-bot/worker/admin/payme.service all still have unit files
# in /etc/systemd/system (dated Sep 5 / Sep 9) and all four are `disabled`; the four bayram-*
# units are `enabled` and `active running`. `systemctl enable hbd-bot.service` would therefore
# still have RESOLVED. These grants are gone by POLICY — the rollback window they existed for
# closed on 2026-09-14 — and not because they pointed at nothing. Re-adding them because "the
# unit files are still there" would reopen a passwordless path to swapping the running fleet
# for a five-day-old one.
#
# ============================================================================
# INSTALL SAFELY — a malformed sudoers file can lock every account out of root
# ============================================================================
#   scp deploy/sudoers.d/bayram-deploy aizu:/tmp/bayram-deploy
#   ssh -t aizu 'sudo visudo -cf /tmp/bayram-deploy'      # must print "parsed OK"
#   ssh -t aizu 'sudo install -m 0440 -o root -g root /tmp/bayram-deploy /etc/sudoers.d/bayram-deploy'
#   ssh -t aizu 'sudo -n -l'                              # confirm the new verbs are listed
#
# Keep a second root shell open until `sudo -n -l` answers. On this host a broken sudoers file
# is not recoverable over ssh: /root/.ssh/authorized_keys is zero bytes and sshd reports
# `permitrootlogin without-password`, so recovery means the VPS provider's console.
#
# Or, to make this edit and the hbd-deploy removal in one validated, backed-up, reversible
# step: deploy/prune-stale-sudo-grants.sh. It does the visudo check before anything lands and
# rolls itself back if a representative verb stops resolving.
#
# ============================================================================
# FNMATCH, AND WHY THE TRAILING `*` HAD TO GO (changed 2026-09-19)
# ============================================================================
# THIS FILE USED TO CONTAIN:
#
#     Cmnd_Alias BAYRAM_LOGS = /usr/bin/journalctl -u bayram-payme *, ...
#
# That is not "journalctl with one more argument". sudo (1.9.15p5 here) joins the user's argv
# into ONE string and fnmatches the whole sudoers argument string against it, so a trailing `*`
# matches ALL remaining arguments. `journalctl -u bayram-payme <anything>` was granted as root:
# a second `-u` for any other unit on the box, `--rotate`, `--vacuum-size=`, and — because sudo
# pages its output and `env_reset` leaves the default pager in place — `less`, which has a `!sh`
# shell escape. A diagnostic-looking grant was a root shell.
#
# That is precisely the hazard deploy/sudoers.d/bayram-release:57-60 refuses to grant. The two
# files contradicted each other; this one yielded.
#
# Explicit verbs are still written out one per line rather than as `{enable|disable|...}` brace
# alternation: fnmatch does not expand braces, so the compact form is not portable even where a
# particular build appears to accept it.
#
# ============================================================================
# WHAT WAS REMOVED ON 2026-09-19, AND THE EXACT LINE TO PUT EACH ONE BACK
# ============================================================================
# Each of these was measured, not assumed. None is re-added without a reason written down.
#
# 1. Cmnd_Alias HBD_ROLLBACK (10 verbs) and the two `hbd-*` vectors in UNITS_NOW.
#    Reason: the rollback window closed 2026-09-14 (above). Policy, not breakage.
#
# 2. `/usr/bin/systemctl status bayram-{bot,worker,admin,payme}.service`.
#    Reason: the pager-shell-escape above, and it is NOT LOAD-BEARING — verified on the host
#    2026-09-19 that `systemctl status bayram-payme` works UNPRIVILEGED for `developer` and
#    prints the same output. `is-active` is kept, and is what the scripts actually call.
#
# 3. Cmnd_Alias BAYRAM_LOGS (`journalctl -u bayram-* *`), removed outright rather than
#    tightened. Reason: also NOT LOAD-BEARING. `developer` is in group `adm`
#    (uid=1000 ... groups=1000(developer),4(adm),24(cdrom),27(sudo),...), and an unprivileged
#    `journalctl -n 1 -u bayram-payme` was run on the host 2026-09-19 and returned a log line.
#    The grant bought nothing but the wildcard.
#
# 4. Cmnd_Alias BAYRAM_CONF = /usr/bin/tee /etc/bayram/payme.env.
#    Reason: NOT an escalation — four checks say so, and they are recorded here so nobody
#    re-adds it believing the survey only found clutter. /etc/bayram is root:root 0755, so it
#    cannot be symlink- or directory-swapped by the operator; the argument is exact, so the
#    write target cannot be redirected; /usr/bin/tee is root:root; and the consumer,
#    bayram-payme.service, runs `User=hbd Group=hbd` with `NoNewPrivileges=true` and reads the
#    path via `Environment=BAYRAM_PAYME_ENV_FILE=`, not systemd `EnvironmentFile=`. Writing it
#    yields config for a non-root service, not root.
#    It goes anyway, for two INTEGRITY reasons. (a) The file is root:hbd 0640 and holds the
#    Payme merchant verification key; this was a standing, passwordless ability to REPLACE that
#    key, and whoever sets it can forge inbound Payme callbacks against our own ledger. (b) If
#    the file is ever absent, `tee` creates it fresh at 0666 & umask — typically 0644 — so the
#    grant could silently publish the cashbox key world-readable. The file exists today, so (b)
#    is latent, not current.
#    Put back with:  Cmnd_Alias BAYRAM_CONF = /usr/bin/tee /etc/bayram/payme.env
#                    (and add BAYRAM_CONF to the developer line at the bottom)
#
# 5. `/usr/bin/tee /etc/caddy/Caddyfile`, which came from hbd-deploy and is deliberately NOT
#    re-homed below with the other three caddy verbs. Reason: it is the same class of grant as
#    (4) and a wider one — an unattended root write of arbitrary content into the public front
#    end of the Payme callback path. Caddy runs `User=caddy`, so it is not root, but it can be
#    made to proxy or file_server anything the caddy user can read. No script in this repo
#    calls it; it is a human verb, and a human still has `(ALL:ALL) ALL` WITH A PASSWORD.
#    Put back with:  developer ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/tee /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
#    If it is put back, pair it with the `caddy validate` verb below in the runbook — a
#    passwordless write with no check in front of it is how the front end goes down at 03:00.
#
# NOT CARRIED FORWARD, AND STILL NOT — the 2026-09-10 hazard:
#   (root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/bash /opt/hbd/deploy-payme.sh
# That grant WAS deleted from hbd-deploy (confirmed absent 2026-09-19). Its target is still
# `developer:developer 0755` inside a `developer`-owned /opt/hbd, so the shape is intact should
# anyone re-add it. THE TARGET MUST NOT BE WRITABLE BY THE ACCOUNT BEING GRANTED —
# deploy/sudoers.d/bayram-release states the rule and why /opt/bayram is root:root.

Cmnd_Alias BAYRAM_UNITS = \
  /usr/bin/systemctl enable bayram-bot.service, \
  /usr/bin/systemctl enable bayram-worker.service, \
  /usr/bin/systemctl enable bayram-admin.service, \
  /usr/bin/systemctl enable bayram-payme.service, \
  /usr/bin/systemctl disable bayram-bot.service, \
  /usr/bin/systemctl disable bayram-worker.service, \
  /usr/bin/systemctl disable bayram-admin.service, \
  /usr/bin/systemctl disable bayram-payme.service, \
  /usr/bin/systemctl start bayram-bot.service, \
  /usr/bin/systemctl start bayram-worker.service, \
  /usr/bin/systemctl start bayram-admin.service, \
  /usr/bin/systemctl start bayram-payme.service, \
  /usr/bin/systemctl stop bayram-payme.service, \
  /usr/bin/systemctl restart bayram-bot.service, \
  /usr/bin/systemctl restart bayram-worker.service, \
  /usr/bin/systemctl restart bayram-admin.service, \
  /usr/bin/systemctl restart bayram-payme.service, \
  /usr/bin/systemctl is-active bayram-bot.service, \
  /usr/bin/systemctl is-active bayram-worker.service, \
  /usr/bin/systemctl is-active bayram-admin.service, \
  /usr/bin/systemctl is-active bayram-payme.service

# `--now` is a flag on enable/disable, so it forms distinct argument vectors and must be matched
# separately. Written out rather than globbed: a trailing `*` on a systemctl rule would match any
# unit on the box, which is the whole thing this drop-in is trying not to do.
Cmnd_Alias UNITS_NOW = \
  /usr/bin/systemctl enable --now bayram-bot.service bayram-worker.service bayram-admin.service bayram-payme.service, \
  /usr/bin/systemctl disable --now bayram-bot.service bayram-worker.service bayram-admin.service bayram-payme.service

# RE-HOMED FROM hbd-deploy, 2026-09-19 — the only grants in that file that are still live.
# caddy.service is `active running`; /etc/caddy/Caddyfile was last edited 2026-09-18 15:16; it
# terminates pay.bayrambot.uz -> 127.0.0.1:8091 (the Payme gateway) and admin.bayrambot.uz ->
# 127.0.0.1:8080. The Cloudflare tunnel delivers to Caddy on 127.0.0.1:80, so "all public
# traffic goes via the tunnel, not Caddy" describes the ENTRY POINT and not the local hop.
# Deleting hbd-deploy without these three lines present here takes the front end's operational
# verbs with it.
#
# All three argument vectors are exact and wildcard-free, so the fnmatch class of hole above
# does not apply. `validate` is read-only and is the check that belongs in front of a reload;
# neither reload nor restart can be handed content by the caller.
Cmnd_Alias BAYRAM_CADDY = \
  /usr/bin/systemctl reload caddy.service, \
  /usr/bin/systemctl restart caddy.service, \
  /usr/bin/caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile --adapter caddyfile

developer ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: BAYRAM_UNITS, UNITS_NOW, BAYRAM_CADDY
developer ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl daemon-reload
